[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: The tab strip is a vocabulary the canon declares, and many narratives across many systems may join one tab

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-TABS-ARE-A-DECLARED-VOCABULARY
TITLE: The tab strip is a vocabulary the canon declares, and many narratives across many systems may join one tab
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: A view needs a tab no row of the table can name, or two views over the same canon need different strips.
STATEMENT: >>>
The tab strip is a DECLARED VOCABULARY, not a shape read off the tree. Its
words are the rows of [LINK: NAR-CANON-TABS], a durable table in the spec: a
tab's key, the label the strip shows, and what the tab is for.

A narrative joins a tab by naming that tab's key in its TAGS -- the same way a
narrative already names a query word, `term`, or `systems`. MANY narratives, in
many systems, may name the SAME tab, and the tab's screen is the union of all
of them: every contribution whose system is switched on, grouped by system, in
the order the systems table declares. That is what makes the page the sum of
the selected systems rather than one page per document.

PLACE is unchanged for `screen`, `section` and `card`, and keeps the meaning
[LINK: DEC-NARRATIVE-DECLARES-ITS-PLACE] gave it. What changes is `tab`: a
narrative with `PLACE: tab` is a CONTRIBUTION to the tab its TAGS name, not a
tab of its own, and its TITLE is a heading inside that tab rather than the
strip's label.

Two tabs are the page's own and are not rows of the table. NODES is the index
over every node in the selection. START is the aggregate opening: EVERY root
narrative lands there, its own body, its cards and sections, and an index of
its screens, grouped by system -- so a root is no longer a tab, and a system
with no root narrative at all is still reachable through the tabs its
narratives join and through Nodes. A tab drops out of the strip when no enabled
system contributes to it.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
[LINK: DEC-NARRATIVE-DECLARES-ITS-PLACE] fixed the right problem -- the second
render guessed placement from tree depth -- but left tab IDENTITY as the
narrative's own UID, so two systems could not contribute to one tab. The strip
was therefore the canon root's tab children plus one entry per other root, and
documenting a new part of the repository added a tab instead of filling the
tabs already there.

The measured tell was already in the data: eleven narratives declared
`PLACE: tab` and carried only SEVEN distinct titles. Edges appeared three
times, Types twice, Words twice -- the same five categories, re-declared once
per document, because there was no way to say "this is the Edges tab" that two
documents could both say. The stable vocabulary a reader wants was present and
had no key to group on.

Naming the tab in TAGS costs no grammar change and no payload field: TAGS is
already free text, already emitted verbatim, and already the carrier for every
other word the view reads off a narrative. Putting the vocabulary in a spec
table rather than in the renderer keeps
[LINK: REQ-VIEW-RENDERER-IS-CONTENT-BLIND]: the page knows that a tabs table
exists, exactly as it knows a systems table exists, and knows none of its words.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Ruled 2026-08-30 as a first-pass decision under the operator's standing
delegation, on their reading of the render: "the semantic layer is what I was
expecting just with like more dynamic front end. the sum of all selected
systems." Overrule by superseding. AUTHORED_BY reads llm because the writer has
no flag for the human rungs.

The five words are v1's, minus the two the page owns: v1 hard-coded
start, types, edges, topics, gaps, nodes, words. What was a literal in a
renderer is a table in the canon here, so a sixth tab is an edit to the canon.

Open, and deliberately not decided here: whether a tab that aggregates two
systems shows one legend or one per system. Every system's legend words differ
today and their colours collide, so the first tab to aggregate two of them will
make the question concrete.
<<<
